{
  "image_name": "04d4308853b75b57c858bf7643980901.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how rice management and soil carbon alter methane emissions by shifting root inputs, oxygen status, and microbial activity in flooded soils. The top half contrasts continuous flooding with intermittent irrigation in terms of increasing harvest index. Under continuous flooding, methane production appears suppressed overall, with reduced rhizodeposition and fewer methanogens. Under intermittent irrigation, added oxygen enters the soil, raising redox conditions and limiting methanogens, so methane release remains roughly unchanged or only slightly affected despite the same reduced rhizodeposition.\n\nThe bottom half focuses on increasing plant biomass and compares low- versus high-carbon soils. In both cases, larger plants increase rhizodeposition and root oxygen transport. In low-carbon soil, the extra plant-derived substrates stimulate methanogens and methane formation more strongly than methane oxidation, leading to higher methane emissions. In high-carbon soil, methanotrophs appear to become more important, likely because oxygen availability near roots supports oxidation, so methane emissions decline or stay lower even though rhizodeposition is increased.\n\nOverall, the diagram highlights that methane outcomes in rice fields depend on the balance between carbon supplied by roots, oxygen entering the rhizosphere, and the relative activity of methanogens versus methanotrophs under different water and soil-carbon conditions.",
